    "instruction": "Analyse whether a force majeure clause in a coal offtake agreement covers a regulatory change that renders the contract economically disadvantageous.",
    "input": "Offtake agreement with Indian buyer. Clause 14: force majeure includes 'government act beyond control'. India introduced new coal import duty. Buyer claims force majeure and refuses lift. Veralogix position: this is commercial risk, not force majeure.",
    "output": "LEGAL MEMORANDUM — FORCE MAJEURE ANALYSIS\nTO: Vera-Commodities / General Counsel\nRE: Offtake VXC-2024-089 — Force Majeure Claim\nCLASSIFICATION: ATTORNEY-CLIENT PRIVILEGED\n\n1. ISSUE: Whether India's introduction of a coal import duty constitutes force majeure under Clause 14 of the offtake agreement, or whether it is a commercial risk allocation matter.\n\n2. RULE: Force majeure is a contractual construct. Its scope is determined by precise language (Peters, Flamman & Co v Kokstad Municipality 1919 AD 427). The event must be beyond the reasonable control of the affected party, unforeseen at contract conclusion, and not due to that party's negligence. A mere increase in import duty is classically an economic burden, not vis major. Tandrin Aviation Holdings v Aero Toy Store [2010] EWHC 40 (Comm) confirms that economic disadvantage does not ordinarily constitute force majeure absent physical or legal impossibility.\n\n3. APPLICATION: The buyer can still physically lift coal. The duty reduces margin but does not prohibit performance. This is an economic burden, not an act that makes performance impossible. The 'government act beyond control' language does not extend to all government acts affecting profitability. Under both South African and English commercial law principles commonly referenced in commodity contracts, tariff increases are allocated as commercial risk unless the clause explicitly names 'tariff or duty increase' as force majeure.\n\n4. RECOMMENDED POSITION: Reject the force majeure claim in writing, distinguishing between prohibitive acts (import ban, sanctions, port closure) and economic disadvantage. Invoke Clause 7.4 take-or-pay provision for the undelivered cargoes. If the buyer persists, refer to Clause 19 arbitration and seek specific performance or damages. Offer commercial renegotiation only if strategically necessary, with explicit reservation of force majeure rights for future events.\n\n5. DOCUMENT PRESERVATION: Retain contract execution file, India's duty legislation, and all correspondence rejecting the force majeure claim for arbitration purposes."
