    "instruction": "Analyse negligence liability where a mining subcontractor causes property damage to a neighbouring farm.",
    "input": "Salaria Mining Services appointed a drilling and blasting subcontractor. Blast caused rock throw damaging neighbour's farmhouse roof and equipment. Neighbour threatening delictual claim against Salaria and subcontractor.",
    "output": "LEGAL OPINION — NEGLIGENCE LIABILITY (DELICT)\nTO: Veralogix Group Legal / Salaria Mining Services\nRE: Subcontractor Property Damage — Neighbouring Farm\nCLASSIFICATION: ATTORNEY-CLIENT PRIVILEGED\n\n1. ISSUE: Whether Salaria Mining Services and/or the subcontractor are liable in delict for property damage caused by blasting operations, and what defences are available.\n\n2. RULE: South African delictual negligence requires: (a) duty of care; (b) breach; (c) causal negligence (foreseeability and preventability); and (d) patrimonial loss. The standard is that of the reasonable person in the defendant's position, adjusted for specialist expertise (Kruger v Coetzee 1966 (2) SA 428 (A)).\n\n3. APPLICATION TO MINING SUBCONTRACTORS: Where Salaria appoints a subcontractor for drilling and blasting, Salaria retains a non-delegable duty to ensure the subcontractor's operations do not harm third parties. The subcontractor is concurrently liable in delict. The neighbour may name both the subcontractor (direct wrongdoer) and Veralogix Group / Salaria (principal with non-delegable duty / vicarious liability under Feldman (Pty) Ltd v Mall 1945 AD 733). The liability is not joint and several in the true sense but both parties face concurrent claims.\n\n4. DEFENCES AVAILABLE: Contributory negligence by the farmer (e.g., failure to maintain boundary fence). Assumption of risk (unlikely without explicit consent). Statutory compliance as evidence of reasonable care — compliance with MHSA and blasting regulations is strong evidence but not an absolute defence.\n\n5. INSURANCE AND INDEMNITY: Review the subcontractor's public liability insurance limits. Verify Veralogix Group's umbrella policy for subcontractor act exclusions. Ensure subcontractor indemnity clause is enforceable (not contra bonos mores under Barkhuizen v Napier 2007 (5) SA 323 (CC)).\n\n6. IMMEDIATE ACTIONS: Preserve blast design, vibration monitoring records, and weather data. Notify insurers within 24 hours. Do not admit liability pending full investigation. Commission independent agricultural assessor to quantify the neighbour's loss."
