    "instruction": "Advise on POPIA compliance requirements for processing personal information within Veralogix Group legal department case files.",
    "input": "Legal team maintains case files containing client personal information, employee records, and witness statements. Need to understand lawful processing conditions and information security safeguards under POPIA.",
    "output": "LEGAL MEMORANDUM — POPIA COMPLIANCE FOR LEGAL CASE FILES\nTO: Veralogix Group Legal / Veramani (Data Protection)\nRE: POPIA Processing Conditions and Security Safeguards\nCLASSIFICATION: ATTORNEY-CLIENT PRIVILEGED\n\n1. ISSUE: Whether Veralogix Group legal department's processing of personal information in case files complies with the Protection of Personal Information Act 4 of 2013 (POPIA), and what safeguards are mandatory.\n\n2. RULE: POPIA establishes eight conditions for lawful processing of personal information (Chapter 3, Sections 11-39): accountability, processing limitation, purpose specification, information quality, openness, security safeguards, data subject participation, and further processing limitation. Special personal information (Section 26) including criminal behaviour and trade union membership requires heightened protection.\n\n3. APPLICATION TO LEGAL CASE FILES:\n- Lawfulness of processing: Processing is necessary for the legitimate interest of the Group (litigation, compliance, risk management) provided it does not unjustifiably infringe on the data subject's privacy. Consent is preferable but not always obtainable; alternative legitimate grounds include compliance with legal obligation and protection of legitimate interests.\n- Information quality: Personal data in case files must be accurate, complete, and not misleading. Regular review protocols are required.\n- Security safeguards (Section 19): Appropriate technical and organisational measures must prevent loss, damage, unauthorised destruction, and unlawful access. This includes encryption of digital files, access control via Veramani's IAM systems, physical security via Aiguille Security, and regular penetration testing.\n- Data subject participation: Data subjects have the right to request access and correction under Section 23, subject to attorney-client privilege and litigation privilege exemptions.\n\n4. RECOMMENDED ACTIONS: (a) Conduct POPIA gap audit of all legal department filing systems. (b) Implement classification scheme distinguishing privileged legal material from administrative personal data. (c) Ensure all digital case files are stored on Veramani-secured servers with encryption at rest and in transit. (d) Limit access to case files on need-to-know basis with audit trails. (e) Register Information Officer with the Information Regulator if not already completed. (f) Review and update PAIA/POPIA manual for public availability.\n\n5. CROSS-SUBSIDIARY COORDINATION: Veramani owns the technical security architecture. Aiguille Security provides physical access control. Legal owns data classification and privilege determinations. IO Green coordinates any environmental whistleblower data subject protections."
