    "instruction": "Analyse the requirements for avoiding an unfair dismissal claim under the LRA where a mine employee is dismissed for gross negligence causing safety incidents.",
    "input": "Employee: senior plant operator at CPI. Incident: bypassed lock-out procedure causing R800k equipment damage. No injury. Previous written warning for similar conduct 18 months ago. Disciplinary hearing scheduled. Employee represented by union shop steward.",
    "output": "LEGAL MEMORANDUM — UNFAIR DISMISSAL RISK ANALYSIS\nTO: CPI / Group Legal\nRE: Disciplinary Dismissal — Gross Negligence\nCLASSIFICATION: ATTORNEY-CLIENT PRIVILEGED\n\n1. ISSUE: Whether dismissal of a senior plant operator for gross negligence causing equipment damage is substantively and procedurally fair under the Labour Relations Act 66 of 1995, considering previous warning and safety context.\n\n2. RULE: Section 188(1)(b) LRA requires dismissal to be both substantively fair (valid reason) and procedurally fair (fair procedure). For misconduct, the employer must prove on balance of probabilities: (a) the employee committed the misconduct; (b) the misconduct is serious enough to justify dismissal (Schedule 8 Item 3(b) Code of Good Practice: Dismissal). Procedural fairness requires investigation, charge, opportunity to respond, and impartial chairperson. Mitigating and aggravating factors must be considered. Previous final warnings can be considered if relevant and not too remote.\n\n3. APPLICATION: Substantive fairness is strong. Bypassing lock-out procedure is wilful disregard of safety protocol, not mere negligence. The R800k damage demonstrates material consequence. Previous written warning for similar conduct 18 months ago is not stale for a repeat safety violation; courts accept longer intervals for safety-critical misconduct. However, mitigating factors include: long service record, clean record except prior incident, no injury, and potential provocation or workload pressure.\n\n4. PROCEDURAL REQUIREMENTS: Ensure the disciplinary hearing chairperson is independent and trained. Allow the employee to call witnesses and present evidence. The union shop steward may represent but not dictate outcome. Provide written reasons. Consider lesser sanction (final written warning, demotion, reassignment) and explain why dismissal is appropriate.\n\n5. RECOMMENDED ACTIONS: (a) Ensure lock-out procedure is documented and the employee was trained. (b) Obtain signed acknowledgement of training. (c) Investigate whether the procedure is routinely bypassed by other operators — if so, systemic failure may weaken the individual culpability argument. (d) Follow exact Schedule 8 procedure. (e) Prepare detailed written reasons for dismissal if that's the outcome. (f) Ensure appeal route to higher management is available."
  },
